17592186045417] [17592186552388 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186552217 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552388 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552388 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552388 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186552077 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552388 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e80bed56-d839-5172-b2e4-e427d055adaa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552397 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186551754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552397 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186551820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552397 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186552089 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552397 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186552117 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552397 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186552194 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552397 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552397 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552397 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186552194 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552397 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f4776dc4-83d1-5d85-abfe-a8e8c4de2f26" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552409 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186552267 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552409 #edb/ref 1069 "q-7661" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552409 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552409 #edb/ref 1071 "A polynomial $f(x)$ is defined as $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+34x-24.$ Given that $f(4)=0$, factor $f(x)$ completely." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552409 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "148f1fdf-ffa7-5ddd-8683-94b6614f6d0f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552409 #edb/ref 1074 "If $f(4)=0$, then by the factor theorem $(x-4)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ To factor $f(x)$ we need to find its other factors.\n\nSince $(x-4)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-4)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-4)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $2$ | $-15$ | $34$ | $-24$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $8$ | $-28$ | $24$ |\n|  | $2$ | $-7$ | $6$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+34x-24}{x-4}=2{x}^{2}-7x+6,\n$$\n\nwhich means\n\n$$\n2{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+34x-24=(x-4)(2{x}^{2}-7x+6).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)(2{x}^{2}-7x+6) & =(x-4)(2x-3)(x-2) \\\\  & =(2x-3)(x-2)(x-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552413 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186552397 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552413 #edb/ref 1069 "q-18153" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552413 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552413 #edb/ref 1071 "A polynomial $f(x)$ is defined as $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-25x+12.$ Given that $f(3)=0$, what is the sum of the roots of the polynomial?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552413 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1adb95f2-1de2-5cd2-b372-b57ff2f582da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552413 #edb/ref 1074 "If $f(3)=0$, then by the factor theorem $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ To find the other roots of the polynomial $f(x)$, we need to find the other factors of $f(x).$\n\nSince $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-3)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\enclose{bottom right}{3} & 2 & 1 & -25 & 12 \\\\  &  & 6 & 21 & -12 \\\\  & 2 & 7 & -4 & \\boxed{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-25x+12}{x-3}=2{x}^{2}+7x-4,\n$$\n\nwhich means\n\n$$\n2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-25x+12=(x-3)(2{x}^{2}+7x-4).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n(x-3)(2{x}^{2}+7x-4)=(x-3)(2x-1)(x+4).\n$$\n\nNow that our polynomial is fully factored, we can see that the three roots of the polynomial are\n\n$$\nx=3,\\,x=\\frac{1}{2},\\,x=-4.\n$$\n\nFinally, the sum of the roots is\n\n$$\n3+\\frac{1}{2}-4=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552423 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186552324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552423 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186552363 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552423 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186552377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552423 #edb/ref 1069 "q-326315" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552423 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552423 #edb/ref 1071 "A polynomial $g(x)$ is defined as $g(x)={x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-x+4.$ Given that $g(4)=0$, the roots of the polynomial $g(x),$ arranged in **ascending order**, are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}},$\\,x=$\n{{field-2}},$\\,$ and\n$\\,x=$\n{{field-3}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552423 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "26aef9f4-359f-5b52-aa4f-51a7c5f79b8e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552423 #edb/ref 1074 "If $g(4)=0$, then by the factor theorem $(x-4)$ is a factor of $g(x).$ To find the other roots of the polynomial $g(x)$, we need to find the other factors of $g(x).$\n\nSince $(x-4)$ is a factor of $g(x),$ we know that $(x-4)$ divides $g(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $g(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-4)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $1$ | $-4$ | $-1$ | $4$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $4$ | $0$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-x+4}{x-4}={x}^{2}-1,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-x+4=(x-4)({x}^{2}-1).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n(x-4)({x}^{2}-1)=(x-4)(x-1)(x+1).\n$$\n\nNow that our polynomial is fully factored, we can see that the three roots of the polynomial, arranged in ascending order, are\n\n$$\nx=-1,\\,x=1,\\text{ and}\\,x=4.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552424 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186552257 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552424 #edb/ref 1069 "q-39370" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552424 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552424 #edb/ref 1071 "A polynomial $f(x)$ is defined as $f(x)=4{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}-x+3.$ Given that $f(3)=0$, what is the sum of the roots of the polynomial?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552424 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "349d9aca-faa9-56e3-8806-6dcea184619f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552424 #edb/ref 1074 "If $f(3)=0$, then by the factor theorem $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ To find the other roots of the polynomial $f(x)$, we need to find the other factors of $f(x).$\n\nSince $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-3)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\enclose{bottom right}{3} & 4 & -12 & -1 & 3 \\\\  &  & 12 & 0 & -3 \\\\  & 4 & 0 & -1 & \\boxed{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{4{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}-x+3}{x-3}=4{x}^{2}-1,\n$$\n\nwhich means\n\n$$\n4{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}-x+3=(x-3)(4{x}^{2}-1).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n(x-3)(4{x}^{2}-1)=(x-3)(2x-1)(2x+1).\n$$\n\nNow that our polynomial is fully factored, we can see that the three roots of the polynomial are\n\n$$\nx=3,\\,x=\\frac{1}{2},\\,x=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the sum of the roots is\n\n$$\n3+\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}=3.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552434 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186552313 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552434 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186552355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552434 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186552376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552434 #edb/ref 1069 "q-269983" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552434 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552434 #edb/ref 1071 "A polynomial $f(x)$ is defined as $f(x)={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-10x+8.$ Given that $f(1)=0$, the roots of the polynomial $f(x),$ arranged in **ascending order**, are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}},$\\,x=$\n{{field-2}},$\\,$ and\n$\\,x=$\n{{field-3}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552434 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "47c5c0f9-0372-541a-ab67-517cecd45015" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552434 #edb/ref 1074 "If $f(1)=0$, then by the factor theorem $(x-1)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ To find the other roots of the polynomial $f(x)$, we need to find the other factors of $f(x).$\n\nSince $(x-1)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-1)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-10$ | $8$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-8$ |\n|  | $1$ | $2$ | $-8$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-10x+8}{x-1}={x}^{2}+2x-8,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-10x+8=(x-1)({x}^{2}+2x-8).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n(x-1)({x}^{2}+2x-8)=(x-1)(x-2)(x+4).\n$$\n\nNow that our polynomial is fully factored, we can see that the three roots of the polynomial, arranged in ascending order, are\n\n$$\nx=-4,\\,x=1,\\,x=2.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552456 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186552308 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552456 #edb/ref 1069 "q-112540" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552456 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552456 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $(x+3)$ is a factor of the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+6x+9,$ factor $f(x)$ over the real numbers." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552456 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "73e5f0e5-34ff-58f3-baa3-f951a9265d77" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552456 #edb/ref 1074 "To factor $f(x)$ we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x+3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x+3)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-3$ | $1$ | $4$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-3$ | $-3$ | $-9$ |\n|  | $1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+6x+9}{x+3}={x}^{2}+x+3,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+6x+9=(x+3)({x}^{2}+x+3).\n$$\n\nTo see whether the quadratic polynomial ${x}^{2}+x+3$ can be factored any further, we calculate its discriminant:\n\n$$\n{b}^{2}-4ac={1}^{2}-4(1)(3)=-11<0\n$$\n\nSo, the quadratic polynomial ${x}^{2}+x+3$ cannot be factored over the real numbers since its discriminant is negative. \n\nTherefore, the full factorization of $f(x)$ over the real numbers is given by \n\n$$\n{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+6x+9=(x+3)({x}^{2}+x+3).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552474 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186552281 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552474 #edb/ref 1069 "q-112551" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552474 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552474 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $(x-4)$ is a factor of the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}-7{x}^{2}+13x-4,$ factor $f(x)$ over the rational numbers." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552474 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8fccdf12-2a25-54ff-895d-1b990f2006e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552474 #edb/ref 1074 "To factor $f(x)$ we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x-4)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-4)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-4)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $1$ | $-7$ | $13$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $4$ | $-12$ | $4$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-3$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}-7{x}^{2}+13x-4}{x-4}={x}^{2}-3x+1,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{3}-7{x}^{2}+13x-4=(x-4)({x}^{2}-3x+1).\n$$\n\nTo see whether the quadratic polynomial ${x}^{2}-3x+1$ can be factored any further, we calculate its discriminant:\n\n$$\n{b}^{2}-4ac=(-3)^{2}-4(1)(1)=5>0\n$$\n\nSo, the quadratic polynomial ${x}^{2}-3x+1$ cannot be factored over the rational numbers since its discriminant is not a perfect square.\n\nTherefore, the full factorization of $f(x)$ (over the rational numbers) is given by \n\n$$\n{x}^{3}-7{x}^{2}+13x-4=(x-4)({x}^{2}-3x+1).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552490 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186552286 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552490 #edb/ref 1069 "q-327191" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552490 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552490 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $(x+1)$ is a factor of the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+6x+8,$ factor $f(x)$ over the real numbers.\n\n$f(x)=(x+1)\\cdot ($ \n{{field-1}}$)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552490 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ad3c509d-b392-55c9-8bbd-386b08676ca2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552490 #edb/ref 1074 "To factor $f(x)$ we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x+1)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x+1)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $-8$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-2$ | $8$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+6x+8}{x+1}={x}^{2}-2x+8,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+6x+8=(x+1)({x}^{2}-2x+8).\n$$\n\nTo see whether the quadratic polynomial ${x}^{2}-2x+8$ can be factored any further, we calculate its discriminant:\n\n$$\n{b}^{2}-4ac={2}^{2}-4(1)(8)=-28<0.\n$$\n\nSo the quadratic polynomial ${x}^{2}-2x+8$ cannot be factored over the real numbers since its discriminant is negative.\n\nTherefore, the full factorization of $f(x)$ over the real numbers is given by \n\n$$\n{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+6x+8=(x+1)\\cdot ({x}^{2}-2x+8).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552494 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186552333 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552494 #edb/ref 1069 "q-73990" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592